WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.970
- [Instructor] We are told graph a line

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.490
with the slope of negative two,

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:06.150
that contains the point
four comma negative three.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.310
And we have our little Khan
Academy graphing widget

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.400
right over here, where
we just have to find

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.630
two points on that line,

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.220
and then that will graph the line for us.

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.350
So pause this video and even
if you don't have access

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:18.183
to the widget right now,

00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:20.360
although it's all
available on Khan Academy,

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:23.050
at least think about how
you would approach this.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.900
And if you have paper and pencil handy,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.530
I encourage you to try to
graph this line on your own,

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:30.070
before I work through it
with this little widget.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.730
All right, now let's do it together.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:33.630
So we do know that it contains

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.490
point four comma negative three.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:37.610
So that's I guess you
could say the easy part,

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:38.561
we just have to find the point

00:00:38.561 --> 00:00:41.320
x is four y is negative three.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:44.750
So it's from the origin four
to the right, three down.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.740
But then we have to figure out
where could another point be?

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.980
Because if we can figure
out another point,

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.720
then we would have graphed the line.

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.000
And the clue here is that they
say a slope of negative two.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.150
So one way to think about it
is, we can start at the point

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.577
that we know is on the line,
and a slope of negative two

00:00:59.577 --> 00:01:04.577
tells us that as x increases
by one, y goes down by two.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.760
The change in why would be negative two.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.730
And so this could be
another point on that line.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:14.010
So I could graph it like
this is x goes up by one,

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.070
as x goes from four to five, y will go,

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.870
or y will change by negative two.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:21.860
So why we'll go from negative
three to negative five.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.310
So this will be done, we
have just graphed that line.

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:27.420
Now another way that you could do it,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.400
because sometimes you might
not have space on the paper,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.810
or on the widget to be
able to go to the right

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:35.500
for x to increase, is to go the other way.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.270
If you have a slope of negative two,

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:42.270
another way to think about
it is, if x goes down by one,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.960
if x goes down by one,
then y goes up by two.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.810
'Cause remember, slope is
change of y over change in x.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.532
So you could either say
you have a positive change

00:01:50.532 --> 00:01:54.590
in y of two when x has
a negative one change,

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.400
or you could think of it when
x is a positive one change,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:58.900
y has a negative two change.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.090
But either way notice,
you got the same line.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.160
Notice this line is the same thing,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:06.370
as if we did the first way
is we had x going up by one

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:08.200
and y going down by two,

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:09.743
it's the exact same line.

